# Mycelia Interactive — Agent Rules (`.cursor/agents.mdc`)

This file is for the **Mycelia Interactive** company website project only.
It is distinct from, and does not affect, the `.cursor/agents.mdc` in the **KAIA Wrapper** project, which remains permanently read-only and must never be written to or edited from this or any other session.

Root [`AGENTS.md`](../AGENTS.md) remains the primary source of truth and is immutable to agents unless Jeremy directs otherwise. This file supplements it with project-specific stack and check details. On version control, this file and `AGENTS.md` now say the same thing: **commit and push are mandatory and automatic; Cloudflare Workers Builds auto-deploying on push is accepted, intended behavior, not something to gate.**

## Project overview

**Mycelia Interactive LLC** company website ([myceliainteractive.com](https://www.myceliainteractive.com)).

### Actual stack (from this repo — do not assume other Mycelia projects)

| Layer | In use |
|---|---|
| Framework | Next.js **16.2.x** (App Router), `output: "export"` static export |
| UI | React **19.2.x**, Tailwind CSS **v4**, Framer Motion, `clsx` / `tailwind-merge` |
| Tooling | TypeScript 5.9, ESLint 9 + `eslint-config-next`, Vitest, React Compiler (`babel-plugin-react-compiler`) |
| Hosting | Cloudflare Workers via **Wrangler** (`wrangler.jsonc`); assets from `./out`; Worker entry `workers/signup-api.ts` (D1, AI binding, cron) |
| Package manager | **npm** (`package-lock.json`) |

There is no separate `typecheck` script. TypeScript is enforced via `next build` and, when needed, `npx tsc --noEmit`.

Do not claim tooling that isn't actually installed (e.g. MSW, Prettier, Playwright, axe-core, Lighthouse) as part of the standard check suite unless it has actually been added as a dependency. Verify against `package.json` before stating a testing methodology as fact.

## Mandatory checks after every change

Run and pass before considering a step complete:

1. `npm run build`
2. `npm run lint`
3. `npx tsc --noEmit` (explicit typecheck; not a package.json script)
4. `npm test` (`vitest run`)

Also run any step-specific runtime/visual/mobile checks as appropriate.

## Version control policy (mandatory, automatic)

After every step's checks pass:

1. Commit to **`main`** with a clear, specific message.
2. Push to `origin/main` **immediately and automatically**, without asking and without waiting for a separate "commit and push" prompt.
3. Report a commit/push record every time: hash, message, branch, and confirmed `origin/main` ahead/behind status, verified against actual `git log origin/main` — never asserted from memory or a prior claim.
4. If a previously claimed push cannot be verified against actual `git log origin/main`, say so explicitly rather than asserting it happened.

There is no Workers Builds gate to check and no holding pattern. Cloudflare Workers Builds will auto-deploy on push — that is expected and accepted, not a reason to delay or ask first.

Use GitHub CLI (`gh`) and git for repository operations. Branch is always **`main`** — never `master`.

### Secrets gate (still applies)

Before push, confirm `.gitignore` excludes secrets:

- `.env*` is ignored except `.env.production` (public build-time `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` defaults only)
- Never commit `.env.local`, credentials, PEM keys, or API secrets
- Confirm repo visibility (public/private) before push; this repo may be public

Automated push removes the manual checkpoint that would otherwise catch an accidental secret commit — treat secret hygiene as mandatory precisely because push is no longer gated.

### Manual/out-of-band deploy commands — still require approval

The automatic push flow above is the only deploy path that runs without asking. Separately, these remain forbidden without Jeremy's explicit approval, since they're redundant with or could conflict with the push-triggered pipeline:

- Manually running `npm run deploy` or `wrangler deploy` outside the normal commit/push flow
- Cloudflare dashboard "retry deploy" / promote / rollback actions
- Any MCP tool that publishes a Worker version directly

`npm run deploy:dry-run` (validation only, does not publish) is always fine as part of ordinary verification.

## Branch policy

- Work on **`main` only**
- Never create or use `master`
- Prefer `gh` for GitHub-facing operations (PRs, repo metadata, visibility checks)

## Standing constraints

- Never hardcode client names or sensitive business details into the codebase
- Mobile-first UI and proper error handling on all implementations

